  "documentation": {
    "range": "<p><em>Takydromus tachydromoides</em> is endemic to Japan. This species can be found in Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu, their satellite islands including Sadoshima, Oki, Iki, the Goto Islands, Yakushima, and Tanegashima (Seki and Hikida 2016).</p>",
    "population": "This species is abundant.",
    "habitats": "It inhabits grasslands and shrubs in plains and mountain areas as well as the surrounding villages. This species mainly preys on insects and spiders. The mating season is from March to early August and females lay two to six eggs per clutch in a couple times per year. This species hibernates underground beneath sunny slopes (Seki and Hikida 2016).",
    "threats": "<p>There are no significant threats to this widespread and abundant species.</p>",
    "measures": "No conservation measures are needed for this species.",
    "use_trade": "There is no information on any use of or trade in this species.",
    "rationale": "Listed as Least Concern because it is abundant, widely distributed and occurs in a variety of habitats that are not subject to significant threats across the range as a whole.",
